# Machine Learning: Building Large Language Models

This repository contains structured learning notes about **building Large Language Models (LLMs)**, based primarily on the Stanford CS229 guest lecture presented by Yann Dubois.

It explores how language models are designed, trained, evaluated, aligned and optimized. The repository follows the complete LLM development process—from modelling language as a probability distribution to transforming a pre-trained model into a helpful AI assistant.

## Topics Covered

- Language modelling and probability distributions
- Autoregressive next-token prediction
- Neural language models and Transformer architecture
- Tokenization and Byte Pair Encoding
- Cross-entropy loss and log-likelihood
- Training-data collection, cleaning and deduplication
- LLM evaluation and perplexity
- Standard NLP benchmarks such as MMLU
- Scaling laws and compute-optimal training
- Model-training costs and computational requirements
- Supervised fine-tuning
- Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback
- Reward modelling and PPO
- Direct Preference Optimization
- Human and AI-generated preference data
- Hardware, GPUs and mixed-precision training
- Systems optimization and efficient inference

## Purpose of This Repository

The purpose of this repository is to document my learning journey and develop a structured technical reference for understanding how modern LLMs are built.

The notes are independently written for educational purposes. They are based primarily on the Stanford lecture and supplemented with explanations of standard machine-learning concepts. They are not an official Stanford transcript.

